ebailey@phakt.usc.edu (mailless) writes:

>>In article <bpn.134.724156561@po.CWRU.Edu> bpn@po.CWRU.Edu (Brian Nelson) writes:
>>I've done some pondering on this subject, too.  The main problem I see is that
>>it would be practically impossible to keep the idea of randomly generated
>>dungeons.  At best, they could be generated as the game is put on-line, but 

>I've been pondering this idea too, and I came up with the same problem.
>You'd have a bunch of players starting at the same place.  It would end up
>being a race through the levels to see who could get to the good stuff
>first.  Who wants to join into a game where the first 15 levels are
>already looted anyway?

This doesn't have to be a problem.  Why do the levels have to be generated
all at once?

Why not randomly synchronize with other games.  For example, you're about
to decend to the next level, 4 other people are playing, 2 are already
synchronized on the same level you are about to decend to...
That means there are the following possibilities:
	1) A brand new level n with only you on it.
	2) You sync with one of the other single player level n's
	3) You sync with the level n that already has 2 player's on it.
Just before allowing your player to drop to level n, in any scenerio,
items and monsters are randomly generated.  Since the probability on any
player being next to a "new" item is not especially high, it is possible
the other player(s) would not even know you arrived.

This way, there should be a fair amount of the game that is scene only
by a single player.  This would allow healing, item research/use
etc. and the "race" possibilities would be scarce.



